oracle-rs (Keeper Security fork)
================================

This repository is a fork of `oracle-rs`, a pure-Rust Oracle Database
thin-mode client.

  Upstream project: https://github.com/stiang/oracle-rs
  Upstream version: 0.1.6
  Upstream commit:  4d55e74fb5cc7e5ed5f7d6ccb73869fa13b9dead
  Upstream author:  Stian Grytøyr
  Upstream license: MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see LICENSE-MIT, LICENSE-APACHE)

The fork adds a credential-injection ("external auth") mode so the KeeperDB
Oracle driver can authenticate through Keeper PAM's `keeperdb_proxy` with no
Oracle Instant Client and no Python (python-oracledb) sidecar. Changes are kept
surgical and close to the upstream commit above so future upstream merges stay
clean.

Modifications by Keeper Security relative to upstream 0.1.6:

  1. Pinned the rustls crypto provider to aws-lc-rs (not ring) for the FIPS
     posture, and selected it explicitly via `ClientConfig::builder_with_provider`
     (helper `aws_lc_client_builder` in `transport/tls.rs`) so the crate is safe
     to link in a binary that also pulls rustls' `ring` provider — the
     no-argument `ClientConfig::builder()` panics at runtime when both providers
     are present.
  2. Added proxy-leg TLS certificate verifiers (`AcceptAnyServerCert`,
     `PinnedServerCert` in `transport/tls.rs`): when connecting through a
     credential-injecting proxy that presents a self-signed cert over an
     already-authenticated tunnel, the standard trust-root-chain + hostname
     check is the wrong model. These mirror python-oracledb's
     `_build_proxy_ssl_context` (CERT_NONE, or pin the exact cert with hostname
     matching disabled) while still verifying the handshake signature.
  3a. CONNECT descriptor (`Config::build_connect_string`) now includes a
      `(CID=(PROGRAM=...)(HOST=...)(USER=...))` block (matching python-oracledb)
      so a proxy's raw CONNECT username-injection succeeds. The USER is a
      placeholder the proxy rewrites; harmless/cosmetic for direct use.
  3b. In proxy-inject mode, `build_connect_string` advertises `PROTOCOL=TCP`
      even under TLS (client TLS to the proxy tunnel is driven by `tls_mode`/the
      handshake, not the descriptor). The proxy reads this PROTOCOL to decide
      how to reach the real Oracle behind it; `TCPS` would make it attempt TLS
      to a plain Oracle listener and fail. Mirrors python-oracledb.
  4. Added an "external auth" mode (`Config::proxy_inject`) in which the
     phase-2 O5LOGON message emits a single `AUTH_PROXY_INJECT="1"` key-value
     pair in place of the computed AUTH_SESSKEY/AUTH_PASSWORD credential fields,
     and the client skips verifier generation + server-response verification, so
     `keeperdb_proxy` can inject the real credentials server-side. Stock
     username/password O5LOGON is unchanged and remains the default.

